horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n3. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4f49124-81ec-5f2d-b275-feeb960c2f73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1061 36423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56d03218-9fd1-5cb5-a471-09641e54c0ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1061 36424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5ff3834-5f43-5149-a054-8bfcbb8b3bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1061 36425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2dbc11bb-69e2-5610-9c8e-28e53d57e103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1061 36426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e65742ab-c850-50fd-b1a4-0670ddc16c2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1061 36427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7847754-3ac7-5ffa-af5a-838c5d3e8886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1061 36805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fdb60d4-7e70-551a-9e4a-8bb61625c9cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model\n\n$$\n\\widehat{y} =f(x)\n$$\n\nthe **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - f(x) \\\\\n&= y - y^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph).\n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)}$, our actual value is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}360}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^(^) &= 2.5x + 165 \\\\\n&= 2.5(82) + 165 \\\\\n&= 370\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - y^(^) \\\\\n&= 360 - 370 \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\[math]10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1065 5968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e5cd2f9-494e-5533-98e9-c24f42631dad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1064 "A **residual plot** displays the values of the independent variable along the horizontal axis and the residuals along the vertical axis.\n\nThe scatter plot below represents data from a four-day sample on the number of people visiting a swimming pool and the average daytime temperature, in degrees Celsius. The line $\\ell$ is the corresponding linear regression line.\n\n![](images/62/6228578012fac7e42bedeb7ab7ad5fc9f42432aed8d5751b675df560bc867417.png)\n\nThese residuals for each of the four data points are highlighted below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70c44fe7b8fce43a8962ac104651acced75e10598e865a3b3c80d09f16ce234.png)\n\nIf we calculate the numerical values for the residuals at each data point (from left to right), we get the following results:\n\n$$\n-4, \\quad 8, \\quad -8, \\quad 4\n$$\n\nTo compute our residual plot, we plot each $x$-value and its corresponding residual on some coordinate axes:\n\n![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1065 5969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddf8fe09-fc0b-546d-942f-17a086708a11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1067 "Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)\n\nWe should be aware of the following interpretations for residual plots:\n\n- Any point *above* the horizontal axis represents a *positive* residual. A positive residual means that the regression model *underestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n- Any point *below* the horizontal axis represents a *negative* residual. A negative residual means that the regression model *overestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n\nResidual plots can also be used to assess whether a trend line is a \"good\" fit for the corresponding data:\n\n- If a linear trend line is a good fit for the data, the residuals should be scattered randomly above and below the horizontal axis and be relatively close to zero.\n- In cases where the residuals show some clear pattern, this usually indicates that the data has no linear trend.\n\nThe residuals are scattered randomly about the horizontal axis in our last example. However, they are not close to zero, indicating that the trend line is a weak fit for the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1065 6034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d7794124-93e8-5abb-8a78-4c8884a07547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "510cace3-4d24-50e7-9ee2-e819b9c603a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb186e7b1c1923e7e8b2776b202b1dcd9b930c56583199964e47530805d246d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512101 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512101 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512101 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01dedd08-ded8-5ff9-8bc9-adeb91c83949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512390 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b5cec74-241f-5ece-ab07-506d78ba15ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512409 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512409 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512409 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8627579e-91b5-5b56-a03e-7f43967fceb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512440 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512440 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512440 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "904444cb-2f64-5eb5-9c3b-5a0b71fe680b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512596 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce895cca-e4ca-58bc-9b7e-d4816e68a767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512816 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a743042b-63eb-545c-bdc0-1aab409141d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186525092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186525098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186525099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1045 "Residuals and Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186525099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524363 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1565ea4e-c392-50d1-9414-12d443b010cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524422 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524422 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524422 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "155561b0-11da-52be-a290-8bc7fd41e681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524457 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524532 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524532 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524532 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56a2ddd1-cadc-5a93-9a25-8dddf